<%# How a remembered change becomes a live record, in three steps.
    #
    # A draft-based universe is the one place in this application where pressing
    # **Save** does not change what the universe holds, and every other piece of
    # copy about the workflow is a consequence rather than an explanation: the flash
    # that says a change was *remembered*, the badge on a row, the two controls on a
    # draft. An author who arrives here from that flash has three questions, and a
    # list of remembered changes answers none of them — where a change comes from,
    # why nothing on the pages they edited has moved, and what makes it live.
    #
    # It is rendered on **both** drafts pages — the list and one draft — because the
    # two are the same page for this workflow: the list is where an author finds out
    # something is waiting, and the draft is where they decide about it. One partial
    # and one set of keys means the two cannot disagree, and the copy lives with the
    # pages rather than in a help centre nothing navigates to.
    #
    # It is rendered only where a draft can exist at all (`DraftPreview#available?`
    # is the same gate the sidebar's panel uses). In a `direct` universe a draft is
    # never opened and the steps would describe a workflow that does not happen, so
    # the sentences would be the one true lie on the page.
    #
    # The steps are a plain ordered list in the same `content-surface` the change
    # rows use, with no styling of its own: this is a sentence about a page, not a
    # feature, and a block that looked like a control would ask to be pressed.
    #
    # **The third step is the universe's mode**, because it is the one step that
    # differs: a `wikipedia` author applies the draft and the changes go live, while
    # in a `github` universe the draft is handed to a reviewer and becomes live only
    # when a reviewer approves it. The first two steps are the same either way, which
    # is why only the last one branches — and a guide describing an apply in a
    # universe whose page has no apply would be describing a control that is not
    # there.
    #
    # **One sentence under the steps says what the steps do not: a draft is one
    # line of work.** The three steps answer "how does a change become live" and are
    # silent on "what happens if I want to start something else", which is the
    # question an author who wants a second direction is actually asking (ADR
    # 0027). Everything remembered goes onto one draft, so trying a different
    # direction means closing the first — and the third step is the only thing that
    # closes it. It is stated here rather than only in the flash that follows
    # **Start editing**, because this is the page an author reads before deciding
    # what to do, and the flash is what they read after they have pressed it.
    #
    # It is a paragraph of its own in the same `page-description` the lead uses,
    # rather than a fourth `<li>`: the list is the workflow in order, and this is
    # not a step of it. %>
<% if draft_preview.available? %>
  <div class="content-surface draft-workflow-guide mb-3">
    <div class="p-3">
      <h2 class="h6 mb-1"><i class="bi bi-journal-text me-1" aria-hidden="true"></i><%= t("drafts.guide.heading") %></h2>
      <p class="page-description mt-0 mb-2"><%= t("drafts.guide.lead") %></p>
      <ol class="mb-0 ps-3">
        <li><%= t("drafts.guide.steps.remember") %></li>
        <li><%= t("drafts.guide.steps.review") %></li>
        <li><%= t(Current.universe.github? ? "drafts.guide.steps.submit" : "drafts.guide.steps.apply") %></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="page-description mt-2 mb-0"><%= t("drafts.guide.one_draft") %></p>
    </div>
  </div>
<% end %>